      "content": "Ethiopia calls for shared Red Sea security — The Rio Times | UA.NEWS ua en pl ru cn Email Sign In Sign Up World Ukraine War with Russia Investigations Open questions Cities that survived Kyiv Polytics Business Gambling Agro Energetics IT and Telecom Pharmaceuticals Logistics and transportation Metallurgy Analytics Finance Real estate Technologies Sport Esports proLife Health Culture Beauty Celebrities Autoworld Traveling Around the World +14° Kyiv +10° Warsaw +7° Washington $ 45.47 € 50.96 zł 11.58 World Ukraine War with Russia Investigations Open questions Cities that survived Kyiv Polytics Business Gambling Agro Energetics IT and Telecom Pharmaceuticals Logistics and transportation Metallurgy Analytics Finance Real estate Technologies Sport Esports proLife Health Culture Beauty Celebrities Autoworld Traveling Around the World more… Email Sign In Sign Up en ua en pl ru cn $ 45.47 € 50.96 zł 11.58 +14° Kyiv +10° Warsaw +7° Washington News Around the World Ethiopia calls for shared Red Sea security — The Rio Times Fedir Kryshtovskyi 24 October 2026 18:32 In Ethiopia, National Intelligence and Security Service chief Redwan Hussein called on countries in the region to jointly ensure security in the Red Sea. At a forum in the city of Bahir Dar on October 23, he said that several coastal countries are unable to independently guarantee the security of this maritime route, The Rio Times reports . Statement at the forum in Bahir Dar The speech took place during the Special Envoys’ Dialogue as part of the 12th High-Level Forum on Security in Africa. The event runs from October 23 to 25 under the theme “Pathways to Peace in Africa: Economic Sovereignty and Geopolitical Transformation.” Hussein said that the militarization of the region, competition among foreign states, and renewed attacks on commercial vessels near the Bab el-Mandeb Strait are putting pressure on trade routes. He called for collective action by regional countries with the support of reliable international partners. Ethiopia is landlocked, but, according to Hussein, is fully dependent on the Red Sea for maritime trade. The Bab el-Mandeb Strait connects the Red Sea with the Gulf of Aden and is an important route for vessels traveling between Asia, the Suez Canal, and Europe. More current news is available on the UA.News Telegram channel Telegram . Participants’ positions and dispute with Eritrea UN Secretary-General’s Special Envoy for the Horn of Africa Guang Cong described the situation in the region as a polycrisis. According to him, the wars in Sudan, South Sudan, and Somalia, as well as threats to shipping in the Red Sea, are closely interconnected. The envoys of Germany and France also stressed the need to strengthen multilateral cooperation and support peace initiatives led by countries in the region, as well as economic integration. During the discussion, Ethiopian Prime Minister’s adviser for East Africa Getachew Reda said that Eritrea continues actions that fuel the crisis. Earlier, Eritrea’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs rejected Addis Ababa’s accusations that Eritrean troops had crossed into Ethiopian territory. Read us on Download our app RELATED MATERIALS News Around the World At least seven people to be charged in Thailand over felling 15 trees — Bangkok Post News Around the World US visa refusal rate in South Korea rises to 24.73% — Korea Herald News Around the World Activist Sheffi Paz dies at 74 — Jerusalem Post News Around the World India prepares evacuations over Cyclone Arnab, 570,000 at risk — Dawn News Around the World Syria says a quarter of displaced people have returned from northern camps — Asharq Al-Awsat News Around the World Dugong freed from fan belt near Exmouth — ABC News Australia News Around the World 31.7% of residents under 24 in Goseong County have migrant backgrounds — Korea Herald × World Ukraine Kyiv Polytics Business Technologies Sport proLife Use of materials from the websites https://ua.news/ is permitted only i